>Shouldn't a samurai have the innate ability to (a)pply his wakizashi to
>himself? This was the traditional way for a samurai to commit Hara-Kiri
>(suicide): stabbing the wakizashi into your belly and pulling it up.

This really set off a short chain of thought in my head:
  If you try to move to an square occupied by a monster, you hit it.
  Similarly, if you move onto your pet, under certain situations,
     you hit it.
  (Putting this observation on hold for a second)

When you zap a wand at a monster in a given direction, you hit it
  (I'm talking about attack wands, of course).
If you zap a wand at '.', you hit yourself.

Now, wouldn't this suggest that just typing a command '.',
   under certain situations (the same as when you thwack your pet)
  you might hit yourself?  For example, a character in a truly
  confused state of mind might think:

"'.' says move to the square I'm standing in.
 Gee, there's somebody already in that square!  Attack!"

Whereupon you bravely shoot yourself in the foot.
Naturally, the Samurai would be Very adept at offing
himself.  Also, the Rogue might even get a backstab
bonus (he sure isn't expecting to get hit when he's
attacking).

Now, I realize there are folks (like me) who frequently
use '.' to mean "stand here and heal awhile".  Maybe this
is a case where we'll start to use the "rest_on_space" flag.

